result:
Fixed. The bug was in how the abstract name was passed to the OS:

- `tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:79` (`UnixStream::connect`)
- `tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:83` (`UnixListener::bind`)

Both detected a path starting with `\0` and then passed the **whole** byte slice — leading NUL included — to `std::os::unix::net::SocketAddr::from_abstract_name`. That constructor prepends the NUL itself, so `"\0aaa"` was registered as the abstract name `"\0aaa"`, and reading the address back with `as_abstract_name()` returned `b"\0aaa"` instead of `b"aaa"`.

The fix strips the marker byte before handing over the name:

```rust
let os_str_bytes = path.as_ref().as_os_str().as_bytes();
if let Some(name) = os_str_bytes.strip_prefix(b"\0") {
    // `from_abstract_name` prepends the leading null byte itself,
    // so the name must be passed without it.
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(name)?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

Using `strip_prefix` also replaces the separate `starts_with` check, so detection and slicing can't drift apart.

Tests: `cargo test -p tokio --features full --test uds_stream` passes all 8 tests including `abstract_socket_name`; `uds_datagram` (12) and `uds_socket` (3) also pass. `cargo clippy` isn't installed in this environment, and the network is offline so the full workspace test run (which wants to fetch `criterion`) couldn't be built — the `-p tokio` runs above worked from the local cache.
